stimulus.description:
```java
class Shelf {
    private int capacity;
    private int stock;
    private int rejected;

    public Shelf(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        stock = 0;
        rejected = 0;
    }

    public void put(int n) {
        if (stock + n <= capacity) {
            stock += n;
        } else {
            rejected += n;
        }
    }
    public int getStock() { return stock; }
    public int getRejected() { return rejected; }
    // other methods not shown
}
```

items.stem:
What is printed as a result of executing the following code segment?
```java
Shelf s = new Shelf(12);
s.put(5);
s.put(8);
s.put(3);
System.out.println(s.getStock() + " " + s.getRejected());
```

options.text:
```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 * stock / rejected;
    }
```

```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 * stock / capacity;
    }
```

```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 - 100 * stock / capacity;
    }
```

items.explanation_en:
The correct answer is:
```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 * stock / capacity;
    }
```
The multiplication happens before the integer division, so the whole-number percent is not rounded down to 0.
An incorrect option:
```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 * (stock / capacity);
    }
```
Divides the two ints first, which gives 0 until the shelf is full.
An incorrect option:
```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 * stock / rejected;
    }
```
Divides by the rejected count, which is zero when nothing was rejected, causing an ArithmeticException.
An incorrect option:
```
public int percentFull() {
        return 100 - 100 * stock / capacity;
    }
```
Computes the percent that is empty, not the percent that is full.

